# ---- raw output ----
Usage: strace [ <options> ] [ <thread or team ID> | <executable with args> ]

Traces the syscalls of a thread or a team. If an executable with
arguments is supplied, it is loaded and it's main thread traced.

Options:
  -a             - Don't print syscall arguments.
  -c             - Record and dump syscall usage statistics.
  -C             - Same as -c, but also print syscalls as usual.
  -d <name>      - Filter the types that have their contents retrieved.
                   <name> is one of: strings, enums, simple, complex or
                                     pointer_values
  -e <names>     - Filter the syscalls.
                   <names> is a comma-separated list of names which can be:
                       * a syscall name
                       * %memory for memory mapping related syscalls
                       * %network or %net for network related syscalls
  -f             - Fast mode. Syscall arguments contents aren't retrieved.
  -h, --help     - Print this text.
  -i             - Print integers in decimal format instead of hexadecimal.
  -l             - Also trace loading the executable. Only considered when
                   an executable is provided.
  --no-color     - Don't colorize output.
  -r             - Don't print syscall return values.
  -s             - Also trace all threads spawned by the supplied thread,
                   respectively the loaded executable's main thread.
  -t             - Also recursively trace all teams created by a traced
                   thread or team.
  -T             - Trace all threads of the supplied or loaded executable's
                   team. If an ID is supplied, it is interpreted as a team
                   ID.
  -o <file>      - directs output into the specified file.
  -S             - prints output to serial debug line.
  -g             - turns off signal tracing.
